#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "git-repack(1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:7
msgid "git-repack - Pack unpacked objects in a repository"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:13
msgid ""
"'git repack' [-a] [-A] [-d] [-f] [-F] [-l] [-n] [-q] [-b] [-m] "
"[--window=<n>] [--depth=<n>] [--threads=<n>] [--keep-pack=<pack-name>] "
"[--write-midx]"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:20
msgid ""
"This command is used to combine all objects that do not currently reside in "
"a \"pack\", into a pack.  It can also be used to re-organize existing packs "
"into a single, more efficient pack."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:24
msgid ""
"A pack is a collection of objects, individually compressed, with delta "
"compression applied, stored in a single file, with an associated index file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:27
msgid ""
"Packs are used to reduce the load on mirror systems, backup engines, disk "
"storage, etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:29
#, no-wrap
msgid "OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:31
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-a`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:39
msgid ""
"Instead of incrementally packing the unpacked objects, pack everything "
"referenced into a single pack.  Especially useful when packing a repository "
"that is used for private development. Use with `-d`.  This will clean up the "
"objects that `git prune` leaves behind, but `git fsck --full --dangling` "
"shows as dangling."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:43
msgid ""
"Note that users fetching over dumb protocols will have to fetch the whole "
"new pack in order to get any contained object, no matter how many other "
"objects in that pack they already have locally."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:48
msgid ""
"Promisor packfiles are repacked separately: if there are packfiles that have "
"an associated \".promisor\" file, these packfiles will be repacked into "
"another separate pack, and an empty \".promisor\" file corresponding to the "
"new separate pack will be written."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:49
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-A`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:59
msgid ""
"Same as `-a`, unless `-d` is used.  Then any unreachable objects in a "
"previous pack become loose, unpacked objects, instead of being left in the "
"old pack.  Unreachable objects are never intentionally added to a pack, even "
"when repacking.  This option prevents unreachable objects from being "
"immediately deleted by way of being left in the old pack and then removed.  "
"Instead, the loose unreachable objects will be pruned according to normal "
"expiry rules with the next 'git gc' invocation. See linkgit:git-gc[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:60
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-d`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:65
msgid ""
"After packing, if the newly created packs make some existing packs "
"redundant, remove the redundant packs.  Also run 'git prune-packed' to "
"remove redundant loose object files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:66
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--cruft`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:71
msgid ""
"Same as `-a`, unless `-d` is used. Then any unreachable objects are packed "
"into a separate cruft pack. Unreachable objects can be pruned using the "
"normal expiry rules with the next `git gc` invocation (see "
"linkgit:git-gc[1]). Incompatible with `-k`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:72
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--cruft-expiration=<approxidate>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:76
msgid ""
"Expire unreachable objects older than `<approxidate>` immediately instead of "
"waiting for the next `git gc` invocation.  Only useful with `--cruft -d`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:77
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--max-cruft-size=<n>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:87
msgid ""
"Repack cruft objects into packs as large as `<n>` bytes before creating new "
"packs. As long as there are enough cruft packs smaller than `<n>`, repacking "
"will cause a new cruft pack to be created containing objects from any "
"combined cruft packs, along with any new unreachable objects. Cruft packs "
"larger than `<n>` will not be modified. When the new cruft pack is larger "
"than `<n>` bytes, it will be split into multiple packs, all of which are "
"guaranteed to be at most `<n>` bytes in size. Only useful with `--cruft -d`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:88
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--expire-to=<dir>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:93
msgid ""
"Write a cruft pack containing pruned objects (if any) to the directory "
"`<dir>`. This option is useful for keeping a copy of any pruned objects in a "
"separate directory as a backup. Only useful with `--cruft -d`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:94
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-l`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:97
msgid ""
"Pass the `--local` option to 'git pack-objects'. See "
"linkgit:git-pack-objects[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:98
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-f`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:101
msgid ""
"Pass the `--no-reuse-delta` option to `git-pack-objects`, see "
"linkgit:git-pack-objects[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:102
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-F`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:105
msgid ""
"Pass the `--no-reuse-object` option to `git-pack-objects`, see "
"linkgit:git-pack-objects[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:106
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-q`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:107
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--quiet`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:110
msgid ""
"Show no progress over the standard error stream and pass the `-q` option to "
"'git pack-objects'. See linkgit:git-pack-objects[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:111
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-n`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:117
msgid ""
"Do not update the server information with 'git update-server-info'.  This "
"option skips updating local catalog files needed to publish this repository "
"(or a direct copy of it)  over HTTP or FTP.  See "
"linkgit:git-update-server-info[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:118
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--window=<n>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:119
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--depth=<n>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:127
msgid ""
"These two options affect how the objects contained in the pack are stored "
"using delta compression. The objects are first internally sorted by type, "
"size and optionally names and compared against the other objects within "
"`--window` to see if using delta compression saves space. `--depth` limits "
"the maximum delta depth; making it too deep affects the performance on the "
"unpacker side, because delta data needs to be applied that many times to get "
"to the necessary object."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:130
msgid ""
"The default value for --window is 10 and --depth is 50. The maximum depth is "
"4095."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:131
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--threads=<n>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:133
msgid "This option is passed through to `git pack-objects`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:134
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--window-memory=<n>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:146
msgid ""
"This option provides an additional limit on top of `--window`; the window "
"size will dynamically scale down so as to not take up more than '<n>' bytes "
"in memory.  This is useful in repositories with a mix of large and small "
"objects to not run out of memory with a large window, but still be able to "
"take advantage of the large window for the smaller objects.  The size can be "
"suffixed with \"k\", \"m\", or \"g\".  `--window-memory=0` makes memory "
"usage unlimited.  The default is taken from the `pack.windowMemory` "
"configuration variable.  Note that the actual memory usage will be the limit "
"multiplied by the number of threads used by linkgit:git-pack-objects[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:147
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--max-pack-size=<n>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:156
msgid ""
"Maximum size of each output pack file. The size can be suffixed with \"k\", "
"\"m\", or \"g\". The minimum size allowed is limited to 1 MiB.  If "
"specified, multiple packfiles may be created, which also prevents the "
"creation of a bitmap index.  The default is unlimited, unless the config "
"variable `pack.packSizeLimit` is set. Note that this option may result in a "
"larger and slower repository; see the discussion in `pack.packSizeLimit`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:157
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--filter=<filter-spec>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:168
msgid ""
"Remove objects matching the filter specification from the resulting packfile "
"and put them into a separate packfile. Note that objects used in the working "
"directory are not filtered out. So for the split to fully work, it's best to "
"perform it in a bare repo and to use the `-a` and `-d` options along with "
"this option.  Also `--no-write-bitmap-index` (or the `repack.writebitmaps` "
"config option set to `false`) should be used otherwise writing bitmap index "
"will fail, as it supposes a single packfile containing all the objects. See "
"linkgit:git-rev-list[1] for valid `<filter-spec>` forms."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:169
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--filter-to=<dir>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:179
msgid ""
"Write the pack containing filtered out objects to the directory "
"`<dir>`. Only useful with `--filter`. This can be used for putting the pack "
"on a separate object directory that is accessed through the Git alternates "
"mechanism. **WARNING:** If the packfile containing the filtered out objects "
"is not accessible, the repo can become corrupt as it might not be possible "
"to access the objects in that packfile. See the `objects` and "
"`objects/info/alternates` sections of linkgit:gitrepository-layout[5]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:180
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-b`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:181
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--write-bitmap-index`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:188
msgid ""
"Write a reachability bitmap index as part of the repack. This only makes "
"sense when used with `-a`, `-A` or `-m`, as the bitmaps must be able to "
"refer to all reachable objects. This option overrides the setting of "
"`repack.writeBitmaps`. This option has no effect if multiple packfiles are "
"created, unless writing a MIDX (in which case a multi-pack bitmap is "
"created)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:189
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--pack-kept-objects`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:197
msgid ""
"Include objects in `.keep` files when repacking.  Note that we still do not "
"delete `.keep` packs after `pack-objects` finishes.  This means that we may "
"duplicate objects, but this makes the option safe to use when there are "
"concurrent pushes or fetches.  This option is generally only useful if you "
"are writing bitmaps with `-b` or `repack.writeBitmaps`, as it ensures that "
"the bitmapped packfile has the necessary objects."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:198
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--keep-pack=<pack-name>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:204
msgid ""
"Exclude the given pack from repacking. This is the equivalent of having "
"`.keep` file on the pack. `<pack-name>` is the pack file name without "
"leading directory (e.g. `pack-123.pack`).  The option can be specified "
"multiple times to keep multiple packs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:205
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--unpack-unreachable=<when>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:210
msgid ""
"When loosening unreachable objects, do not bother loosening any objects "
"older than `<when>`. This can be used to optimize out the write of any "
"objects that would be immediately pruned by a follow-up `git prune`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:211
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-k`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:212
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--keep-unreachable`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:217
msgid ""
"When used with `-ad`, any unreachable objects from existing packs will be "
"appended to the end of the packfile instead of being removed. In addition, "
"any unreachable loose objects will be packed (and their loose counterparts "
"removed)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:218
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-i`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:219
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--delta-islands`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:222
msgid ""
"Pass the `--delta-islands` option to `git-pack-objects`, see "
"linkgit:git-pack-objects[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:223
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-g<factor>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:224
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--geometric=<factor>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:228
msgid ""
"Arrange resulting pack structure so that each successive pack contains at "
"least `<factor>` times the number of objects as the next-largest pack."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:234
msgid ""
"`git repack` ensures this by determining a \"cut\" of packfiles that need to "
"be repacked into one in order to ensure a geometric progression. It picks "
"the smallest set of packfiles such that as many of the larger packfiles (by "
"count of objects contained in that pack) may be left intact."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:239
msgid ""
"Unlike other repack modes, the set of objects to pack is determined uniquely "
"by the set of packs being \"rolled-up\"; in other words, the packs "
"determined to need to be combined in order to restore a geometric "
"progression."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:242
msgid ""
"Loose objects are implicitly included in this \"roll-up\", without respect "
"to their reachability. This is subject to change in the future."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:246
msgid ""
"When writing a multi-pack bitmap, `git repack` selects the largest resulting "
"pack as the preferred pack for object selection by the MIDX (see "
"linkgit:git-multi-pack-index[1])."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:247
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-m`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:248
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--write-midx`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:251
msgid ""
"Write a multi-pack index (see linkgit:git-multi-pack-index[1])  containing "
"the non-redundant packs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:253
#, no-wrap
msgid "CONFIGURATION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:257
msgid ""
"Various configuration variables affect packing, see linkgit:git-config[1] "
"(search for \"pack\" and \"delta\")."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:267
msgid ""
"By default, the command passes `--delta-base-offset` option to 'git "
"pack-objects'; this typically results in slightly smaller packs, but the "
"generated packs are incompatible with versions of Git older than version "
"1.4.4. If you need to share your repository with such ancient Git versions, "
"either directly or via the dumb http protocol, then you need to set the "
"configuration variable `repack.UseDeltaBaseOffset` to \"false\" and "
"repack. Access from old Git versions over the native protocol is unaffected "
"by this option as the conversion is performed on the fly as needed in that "
"case."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-repack.txt:271
msgid ""
"Delta compression is not used on objects larger than the "
"`core.bigFileThreshold` configuration variable and on files with the "
"attribute `delta` set to false."
msgstr ""

